Stop an unreachable STUN server from pinning the GNS service thread.

A STUN bind request whose send fails synchronously (unroutable server, no
network, blocked egress) reports the failure through its callback
immediately and deletes itself. The reflexive keepalive callback answers
that by queueing another request to the "next" STUN server -- the same one
again when only one is configured -- whose first send fails identically.
With no floor between attempts this spins hard enough to pin the service
thread, and GNS reports it itself:

    Processed thinkers 10001 times -- probably one thinker keeps requesting an immediate wakeup call.
    SteamNetworkingSockets lock held for 17.2ms.  (Performance warning.)
        ServiceThread,CSteamNetworkingSocketsSTUNRequest::Think(x10000),SendUDPacket(x10000)

Nothing else -- ICE included -- makes progress behind it, so connections
never establish. 1.6.0 fixed the sibling candidate-discovery callback (it
walks the server list once, then marks the interface failed); the
keepalive path still cycles.

Throttled in QueueBindRequest rather than in any single caller, so every
path that can re-issue is covered. Server-reflexive candidates are an
optimization: skipping an attempt is harmless, a later think tries again.


diff --git a/src/steamnetworkingsockets/clientlib/steamnetworkingsockets_ice_client.cpp b/src/steamnetworkingsockets/clientlib/steamnetworkingsockets_ice_client.cpp
index de4b490..b97ebb8 100644
--- a/src/steamnetworkingsockets/clientlib/steamnetworkingsockets_ice_client.cpp
+++ b/src/steamnetworkingsockets/clientlib/steamnetworkingsockets_ice_client.cpp
@@ -1020,6 +1020,25 @@ void ICESessionInterface::QueueBindRequest( const netadr_t &addrSTUNServer, Recv
 {
     Assert( !m_pPendingSTUNRequest );
 
+    // Throttle. A request whose send fails synchronously (unroutable STUN
+    // server, no network, blocked egress) reports the failure through its
+    // callback immediately and deletes itself; the reflexive keepalive
+    // callback answers that by queueing another request to the "next" STUN
+    // server -- the same one again when only one is configured -- which
+    // fails its first send identically. Without a floor between attempts
+    // that spins hard enough to pin the service thread: GNS reports
+    // "Processed thinkers 10001 times" with STUNRequest::Think(x10000),
+    // and nothing else -- ICE included -- makes progress behind it.
+    // Applied here rather than in any one caller so every path that can
+    // re-issue is covered. Server-reflexive candidates are an
+    // optimization, so skipping an attempt is harmless; a later think
+    // tries again.
+    const SteamNetworkingMicroseconds usecNow = SteamNetworkingSockets_GetLocalTimestamp();
+    if ( usecNow < m_usecNextBindRequestAllowed )
+        return;
+    m_usecNextBindRequestAllowed = usecNow + 250000;
+
+
     m_pPendingSTUNRequest = new CSteamNetworkingSocketsSTUNRequest( this );
     m_pPendingSTUNRequest->Queue( k_nSTUN_BindingRequest, nEncoding, addrSTUNServer, cb );
 }
